---

*A Vignette about Cultural Space and Property Value*

When live events began to return after the COVID lockdown, Montez Press Radio still couldn't bring an audience indoors. So they used the fire escape. Musicians played from stairs above the street. People gathered below. At one point during this event, a neighbor came out to complain about the noise. Tom Laprade, one of the founder-directors of Montez Press Radio, remembers shouting back that the neighbor shouldn’t be complaining: their property value was going up because of places like Montez and the noise they make, not despite it. It was an odd exchange: the sound bothered the neighbor, but it also helped make the neighborhood more desirable.

Tom’s response points to something else. Places like Montez bring performances, people, noise, and crowds into a neighborhood. That activity can make the area more difficult to live in for people who are looking for a calm, quiet neighborhood, but it can also make it more desirable, increasing property values. And it is often the case that the value created by these types of cultural activities goes to property owners, while the tenant that helped create it may benefit much less.

