Decades earlier, David Mancuso’s legendary dance party, The Loft, faced a similar tension in a different form. In 1970, David Mancuso had been living at 647 Broadway for four or five years when he held a Valentine’s Day party in his second-floor home. Guests came by invitation, paid a voluntary contribution at the door, and danced through the night. Mancuso sold no alcohol. The parties kept happening. They became known as The Loft.

In 1973, Mancuso expanded into the neighboring loft at 645 Broadway, and his landlord let him remove the brick wall between the two main spaces. The party grew. Neighbors complained about the disruptions, and city officials questioned whether the building could legally support the use. By 1974, Mancuso stopped holding the weekly parties in the Broadway loft. He remained a tenant in the building, but the party itself moved elsewhere.

In 1975, he reopened it at 99 Prince Street, across the street from Fanelli Cafe, one of SoHo’s long-standing bars. The Loft stayed there for almost a decade. Then the building was sold in 1984. Today, 99 Prince Street is a Moncler boutique. By then, SoHo had already become increasingly expensive. Mancuso moved to East 3rd Street, this time into a building he had bought. The landlord problem disappeared. Other problems did not. Drugs, crime, and safety concerns kept many longtime guests away.

Through the 1990s, Mancuso moved The Loft through various locations on Avenue A, Avenue B, East 14th Street, and other East Village addresses. Buildings were sold. Finances tightened. Fewer guests followed the party. The Loft became increasingly itinerant.

In 2001, Mancuso began holding semi-regular Loft parties at the Ukrainian National Home on Second Avenue. There was no permanent Loft anymore, but the Loft community reconstructed the party each time. They brought in the sound system, hung decorations, prepared food, welcomed the crowd, and packed everything away afterward. Each time, they reassembled the room.

Mancuso died in 2016, but The Loft continued.

